        <!-- SECTION 1: Oil vs Water -->
        <div id="oil-vs-water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Oil-Based vs. Water-Based Deck Stain: What Actually Changes
          </h2>

          <p>
            The <strong>oil vs water based deck stain</strong> debate gets treated like a matter of opinion online, but the practical differences are chemical, not stylistic. Oil-based stains use linseed, tung, soy, or blended drying oils as the carrier for pigment and protective additives. Those oils have small molecules that penetrate deep into wood fiber, physically replacing the natural oils that UV exposure and weathering strip out of wood over time. Water-based stains use acrylic or alkyd-emulsion resins suspended in water, which tend to sit closer to the surface and rely more on forming a thin protective layer than on saturating the wood from within.
          </p>

          <p>
            That structural difference cascades into almost every practical comparison homeowners care about. Oil-based stains generally penetrate deeper, which means better long-term water repellency and a finish that <strong>fades rather than peels</strong> as it wears — there's no thick film to crack and lift. Water-based stains clean up with soap and water, dry faster, carry lower VOCs, and have closed much of the historical durability gap with modern nano-particle UV additives. Neither is universally "better" — they're suited to different priorities.
          </p>

