          <p>
            The mildew line in that table deserves a second look, because it surprises a lot of homeowners. A straight linseed-oil carrier is itself an organic material that mildew and mold can feed on in warm, humid, shaded conditions — which is exactly why the better oil-based stains in our test lineup use tung oil, soy oil blends, or add dedicated mildewcide packages rather than relying on raw linseed alone. If you live somewhere hot and humid and are staining a heavily shaded deck, look specifically for an oil-based product with a stated mildewcide additive rather than assuming all oil stains behave the same way.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our take after years of side-by-side testing: for a deck you want to look and perform like a genuinely conditioned, protected surface — and where you're willing to handle mineral spirits for cleanup and a longer cure window — oil-based stain remains the stronger long-term choice. Our broader <a href="/best-deck-stain-reviews/">Best Deck Stain Reviews</a> guide covers water-based and solid options too if you want the full picture across every stain category, not just oil-based.
