          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Types of Oil-Based Deck Stain: Penetrating, Semi-Transparent, and Translucent
          </h2>

          <p>
            "Oil-based deck stain" isn't one product category — it's a chemistry that spans several distinct finish types, and picking the wrong one for your goals is a common source of disappointment. Here's how the three main types actually differ.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Penetrating Oil (Natural Formula)</h3>
          <p>
            A <strong>penetrating oil deck stain</strong> carries little to no pigment and is designed to soak fully into wood fiber, conditioning it and restoring the oils lost to UV oxidation. It shows the maximum amount of natural wood grain and color variation. Products like Penofin Blue Label sit here. Because there's essentially no film at all, failure mode is pure fading — there's nothing to peel. The tradeoff is UV protection: with less pigment to absorb sunlight, these formulas typically need the most frequent reapplication of the three types, especially on horizontal surfaces in full sun.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Semi-Transparent</h3>
          <p>
            Semi-transparent oil stains add a moderate pigment load to the same penetrating-oil base, giving real, visible color while still letting the wood grain show through clearly. This is the most popular category for a reason — it balances color, UV protection, and grain visibility better than either extreme. Messmer's UV Plus, Storm System Category 5, and Wolman's DuraStain all fall in this middle category, and for most residential decks, this is the sweet spot.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Translucent</h3>
          <p>
            Translucent oil-based stains carry the heaviest pigment load of the three while still remaining an oil-based, non-solid product. They obscure more of the fine grain detail in exchange for the most uniform, consistent color of any oil-based option — closer to a furniture-grade finish than a rustic wood-tone look. PPG Proluxe Cetol SRD is the clearest example in our test lineup. If you want your railings and stair treads to look intentional and matched rather than showing every natural variation in the lumber, translucent is the category to reach for.
          </p>

